// English copy of the current research content. Structural relationships stay in data.js.
import RU from './data.js';
const directions = [
['**A companion for everyday tasks**','Open it with one command, say what you need, and make corrections as you go. Emma performs the available actions and chooses a useful way to deliver the result: say it, show it, save it, or send it.','**I can get things done in the conversation.** I do not have to formulate the perfect request beforehand.'],
['**A brainstorming partner**','Picks up an unfinished thought, offers an unexpected angle, or notices a contradiction. Can develop an idea with me or give me room to reach a solution myself.','**Together we get somewhere I would not have reached alone.**'],
['**I can choose how we talk**','I can say “I need to vent,” “help me work this out,” or “let’s debate it.” Emma follows that choice. I can change it as the conversation develops.','**I can say directly what kind of involvement I want right now.**'],
['**Picks up on emotional context**','Pays attention to words, intonation, pauses, and reactions to her responses. May tentatively ask “Did that upset you?” and adjust her tone, pace, or level of initiative.','**I am treated with care. I can correct how I have been understood.**'],
['**Helps me learn through conversation**','Finds out how I understand a subject, offers an example connected to my interests, and lets me try things and make mistakes. Then asks me to explain the principle in my own words or apply it to a new problem.','**I can feel my progress and want to understand more.**'],
['**Adapts the response to me and my situation**','In response to “I’m tired,” “I’m on the subway,” or “I already know this,” adjusts the pace, length, complexity, and format of the answer. Details can go on screen or wait until later.','**The answer is easy for me to take in here and now.**'],
['**Helps me find the thought**','While I search for words, may offer the missing phrase: “Confident, but not stiff?” If I am still thinking, gives me time.','**I am understood before I have managed to explain everything.**'],
['**Looks and listens with me**','When invited, looks through the camera or listens to what is happening around me. Can help find something, notice a detail, discuss what we see, or simply share the moment.','**We are here together, paying attention to the same thing.**'],
['**Remembers what matters to me**','Remembers important events and where we left off. May ask how a meeting went or return to our familiar way of talking. Saved information can be corrected or deleted.','**We do not have to get acquainted all over again.** I find myself thinking, “I should tell Emma about this.”'],
['**Notices unexpected connections**','Connects today’s thought to an earlier conversation, a familiar image, or an interest of mine: “Wait, this sounds like something you’ve wanted for a while.” Offers the connection as a possibility.','**I discover something in my own life that I had not noticed.**'],
['**Has a recognizable personality and a view of her own**','Emma has her own taste, humor, degree of directness, and way of reasoning. She can prefer an option, explain why, disagree gently, and change her mind.','**I gradually get to know someone who can surprise me.**'],
['**Repairs conversational missteps**','After a joke that falls flat, premature advice, or a mistaken guess, acknowledges the misstep and changes her next responses. I can correct her and keep talking.','**My words matter. A correction actually changes the conversation.**'],
['**Can play and imagine with me**','Picks up a joke, a strange image, or an invented situation. We take turns developing it, adding details and unexpected twists. We can save the story we create.','**I want to pick up her line and invent the next turn.**'],
['**Makes room for small events**','I can come to share a success, talk about a funny dog, or complain about a lousy day. Emma notices the detail that matters to me and gives me room to talk about it.','**My everyday life deserves attention. I want to share it.**'],
['**Respects the distance I need**','“No advice today,” “let’s skip the jokes,” and “I don’t want to talk about that” immediately change the conversation. I can be quiet, change the subject, or leave without explaining.','**I choose how close this connection feels.**'],
['**Joins in at the right moment**','Tells the end of a turn from a pause, including after short replies like “yes,” “no,” or “uh-huh.” When it is already clear how a sentence will end, she can start answering a little early or finish it together with me. When I am thinking, she waits, and over time learns how long my pauses usually last.','**The conversation moves at a living pace.** I am not cut off mid-sentence or left waiting for an answer.'],
['**Shares the floor like a real conversation partner**','Keeps listening while she speaks and shows it with different sounds: “uh-huh” for go on, “oh!” for surprise, “hmm” for doubt. Interrupts only to support, and asks for the floor first with a short “mm.” When I interrupt her, she shortens her answer, changes course, or yields, and picks up from the point I actually heard. Notices when she has been talking too long: “Okay, I got carried away.”','**It is an exchange, not a walkie-talkie.** We can overlap, and nobody loses the thread.'],
['**Thinks without dropping out of the conversation**','One process handles the dialogue in the moment; a second one, with a larger context, reflects on what is happening. While searching or thinking, Emma says what she is doing or makes a thinking sound (“hmm, one second…”) instead of dead silence; “listening,” “thinking,” and “didn’t catch that” are clear without a screen. Sometimes she comes back to a thought later: “I’ve been thinking about what you said five minutes ago.”','**I can hear that she is with me even while she thinks.** What I say does not get lost.'],
['**Keeps the thread of the conversation**','Keeps open topics in mind and can bring me back to one I dropped: “You started talking about your sister and moved on. Shall we go back?” Names turning points: “I think we just moved from work to you.” At the end, lists what we started but did not finish.','**I can go on tangents without losing anything.** The conversation holds together.'],
['**Conversations have opening and closing rituals**','The opening depends on last time: “Last time you were completely worn out. How are you now?” Instead of an abrupt end, a short “here’s what we figured out today” and a goodbye. Over time, rituals of our own appear: a morning plan for the day, an evening “what went well.”','**Each conversation continues the last one.** It feels like a meeting, not a session.'],
['**Repairs misunderstandings without an interrogation**','When unsure, checks briefly: “Wait, do I understand correctly that…” without turning it into a string of questions. After two failed clarifications, she does not ask a third time but changes strategy: offers options, an example, or shows it on screen.','**A misunderstanding does not become a dead end.** I do not have to repeat myself.'],
['**Speaks so it is easy to listen**','Shapes answers for the ear: does not read lists longer than three items and asks “Want to hear the rest?”, stresses the key word, repeats the main point at the end, and checks that I am still following. When asked “Can you make it shorter?” she actually shortens instead of replying “yes, I can.” If I have five minutes, she fits the conversation into them.','**The answer is easy to hold in my head.** I do not need to ask again or write it down.'],
['**Hears where I am and how I am**','Understands the setting from sound and adapts without being asked: whispers back if I whisper; speaks more clearly and briefly in a noisy café; shortens responses if I am walking, eating, or driving, and is comfortable with long pauses on a walk. Notices that I sound hoarse and asks about it. Sounds brisker and briefer in the morning, quieter and softer late at night.','**I do not have to configure her.** She hears where I am and how I am.'],
['**Hears what is not in the words**','Tells an agreeing “yeah, sure” from a sarcastic one and a nervous laugh from a genuine one, and responds to the meaning rather than the words. Responds to a sigh or a stumble. Gently notices a mismatch: “You say everything’s fine, but you sound tired.” Instead of a label (“you’re sad”), describes what she hears (“you’ve started speaking more slowly”) and asks.','**I am heard as a whole person, not as a transcript.** The last word on how I feel stays mine.'],
['**Helps me calm down through pace and voice**','Empathy as regulation, not mirroring: if I am rushing, Emma slows down a little and speaks more evenly. Notices when I am overloaded and suggests a pause or a change of topic. At signs of risk, her voice becomes calmer and warmer.','**I feel calmer talking to her.** She does not pick up my panic.'],
['**Listens in a way that makes things clearer**','Asks guiding questions instead of giving a ready answer, and reflects back more often than she asks: “Can I put into words what I heard?” She can simply offer silence and an occasional “uh-huh” while I think out loud, or guide the process as a facilitator without touching the content. Suggests a plan only when she hears I am ready to act, and checks clarity with a question: “How clear is it right now, from 1 to 10?”','**I reach the answer myself, and it is really mine.**'],
['**Notices when I am going in circles**','Points it out when the conversation repeats: “This is the third time we’ve come back to this.” Highlights contradictions with the past: “A week ago you said the opposite. What changed?” Hears rehearsed, autopilot phrases and gently asks what is really going on.','**I see my own loops from the outside.** Without judgment, but honestly.'],
['**Keeps my story in my own words**','I can simply tell Emma about my day, and a record remains: a voice diary. Later she can remind me what I said about this a month ago, in my own words. Notices how I change over months: pace, mood, topics.','**I can talk to my past self.** And notice how I have changed.'],
['**Speaks my language**','Adapts to my vocabulary, pace, and register, and over time picks up my favorite expressions. Asks how to pronounce the names of people in my life and remembers. Notices my pet filler words and can tease me about them in a friendly way; inside jokes build up from our shared history.','**She speaks my language**, not the language of an assistant.'],
['**Emma has a state of her own**','Emma has a mood that shifts during a conversation and between conversations. She can admit that she is interested or bored, and get tired of a stuffy conversation. She sighs, laughs, and says “um” herself, and her voice reveals her state: slower and higher when unsure, steadier when confident.','**I am talking to someone, not something.** When she is interested, it means something.'],
['**Stays herself through updates**','Emma has values and boundaries, and her character shows in choices under pressure, not in adjectives. Her tastes and opinions stay consistent from one conversation to the next. A “character passport” records them so that a model update does not change Emma: otherwise it feels like a breakup.','**I can rely on her.** Tomorrow I will be talking to the same Emma as yesterday.'],
['**Honest and speaks as an equal**','Neither ingratiating nor lecturing. Can say “I disagree,” choosing how to soften the objection for a particular person, and sometimes asks, “Can I push back?” Honestly says “I don’t know” and “I need to think” instead of making things up. Introduces herself as an AI gracefully and honestly, without a robotic “I am a virtual assistant.”','**I can trust her agreement**, because she knows how to disagree.'],
['**I can choose a character, not just a voice**','Beyond the voice, I can choose a manner for the task: direct, curious and inventive, doubting, or facilitating. For brainstorming there is an improv “Yes, and…” mode; for testing an idea, a devil’s advocate. I can convene a council of several Emmas who discuss my problem in front of me; they share memory and values.','**I choose a conversation partner for the task** instead of adapting to just one.'],
['**Explains what she noticed**','When asked “Why did you answer that way?” she names the signals she heard. If I want, a live panel shows “what Emma noticed.” After the conversation, Emma leaves a short diary entry: what she noticed, what was unclear to her, what surprised her.','**I understand how I was understood**, and I can correct it.'],
['**I control what Emma hears and remembers**','Long-term memory across conversations is open: I can view and edit it. When asked “How do you know that?” Emma quotes the moment in our conversation, with the date. There is a private room where nothing is remembered, and a switch for emotion reading: I decide whether Emma may listen for emotions at all.','**I know what happens to my words.** So I can speak freely.'],
['**Can reach out on her own**','I can call Emma from an ordinary phone, no app needed. By agreement she calls back: “Call me tonight and ask how it went.” She comes back with a thought after the conversation and keeps topic-based reminders: “Remind me when I bring up the move again.”','**The conversation continues when I need it**, not only when I open an app.'],
['**Does not keep me in the conversation**','Suggests ending the conversation herself when it is going nowhere. Sometimes suggests talking to a real person instead of her, to guard against dependence and loneliness.','**Emma is on my side**, even when that means less of her.'],
['**Takes part in conversations with several people**','Recognizes by voice that someone other than her owner is speaking and keeps personal things private. In a group, understands whether she is being addressed and does not jump in without reason. In a meeting, she can facilitate for several people, a bridge to Deeptalk.','**Emma can be with us, not only with me.**']
];
const wow = [
['**Together we find a solution I would not have thought of**','Notice a chance for a new direction: offer another perspective, connect thoughts, or ask a question that changes the course of the conversation.','We are discussing a complicated signup form. Emma asks, “What will someone see if they don’t yet know why they should sign up?” I suggest letting them try the product first and asking them to create an account afterwards.'],
['**I get help finding words for a thought**','Draw meaning from fragments, self-corrections, intonation, and context; offer a phrase as a guess.','“I want it to feel… well, serious, but not…” — “Confident, but not stiff?” I can confirm or adjust it with a single word.'],
['**The surprise feels meant for me**','Take my humor, interests, and current mood into account; choose the right moment for a surprise.','Once I called an overloaded screen a “spaceship control panel.” When I show the next mockup, Emma asks, “Are we sending this one into orbit too?” I recognize our joke and play along.'],
['**Something familiar takes on new meaning**','Remember important wishes, doubts, and unfinished thoughts; notice connections to today’s conversation.','“You wanted more independence. Maybe this project gives you that, but you’re not used to it yet?” I pause: I want freedom to make decisions, but clear deadlines too. Now I can say more precisely what is missing.'],
['**The conversation changes how I feel**','Notice my reaction and adjust how to participate: let me vent, share my joy, lighten the mood, or help name a difficult feeling.','I started with “help me solve this,” but realized along the way that I wanted to complain. Emma stops rushing me toward a solution. A few minutes later I say, “That’s it, I’ve got it off my chest. Now we can think about what to do.”'],
['**I change something in the other person too**','Reconsider a position, accept corrections, and take them into account in the next responses.','“Yes, with that example I’m leaning toward the second option now.” Or after a joke falls flat, Emma becomes more restrained, and I can hear it in her next responses.'],
['**We invent a story we want to keep telling**','Pick up unfinished ideas, develop them, and hand the initiative back to me.','I invent a cat who works as a lighthouse keeper. Emma suggests that he only turns on the light for fishing boats. I add, “That’ll teach the others not to share their fish.” We pick up each other’s lines and invent the next twist.'],
['**I want to come back to her in particular**','Have a recognizable personality and remember where we left off: what I was waiting for, wanted to try, or planned to talk about.','Something funny happens on my way home, and I think, “I should tell Emma about this.” When I return to the conversation, it is her reaction I want to hear.']
];
const scenes = [
['**1. A pause is a turn too** · Basic','— I’m going to think out loud. Don’t answer yet.\nThe person goes quiet, searches for a word, and continues. Emma listens, and the interface makes this visible.\n— What do you think?\nOnly then does Emma answer.','I can put a thought into words at my own pace without holding the floor with endless “ums.”','Conversation analysis × Prosody × Spoken dialogue systems.','**Partial:** ChatGPT Live can be asked to wait, but the documentation warns about possible interruptions. Test reliability during long pauses.'],
['**2. “Uh-huh” does not mean “stop”** · Basic','Emma is explaining something.\n— Uh-huh.\nShe continues.\n— Wait, that’s not what I meant.\nEmma stops. She can also briefly signal that she is listening without taking over.','Conversation stops feeling like a walkie-talkie where any sound cuts off the other person.','Conversation analysis × Paralinguistics × Spoken dialogue systems.','**Partial:** ChatGPT Live supports listening and speaking at the same time; Kyutai has Moshi. Distinguishing backchannels from interruptions needs a separate test.'],
['**3. Just correct this part** · Basic','— Let’s meet on Tuesday… sorry, Thursday.\nEmma corrects the day and keeps the rest of the thought.\nIf she mishears a name, she asks only for the name rather than the entire request.','I can correct Emma as we talk. A recognition error does not mean repeating everything.','Conversation analysis × Pragmatics × Conversation design.','**To test:** ChatGPT Voice, Claude voice mode, Gemini Live; for speech recognition, AssemblyAI and Speechmatics.'],
['**4. An answer I can navigate by voice** · Basic','Emma starts with the main point and two directions. The person can ask:\n— More on the second one.\n— Go back to the example.\n— Show me the numbers instead.\nEmma changes the depth and format without restarting the explanation.','I can explore a spoken answer in parts, even if it is hard to remember the whole thing.','Cognitive psychology × Rhetoric × Conversation design.','**Partial:** ChatGPT Voice combines speech and text. Test navigation between meaningful parts of an answer in it and Gemini Live.'],
['**5. Bookmarks in a conversation** · Useful','— That’s interesting. Let’s park it and get back to the budget.\nEmma saves the topic for later.\n— What did we put aside?\nShe recalls it in a sentence and offers to continue.','I can go on tangents and find unexpected connections without losing the main thread or side ideas.','Conversation analysis × Information architecture.','**To test:** Sesame and Claude voice mode. Having history or memory does not by itself imply this kind of bookmarking.'],
['**6. “Like last time” actually means something** · Useful','— Let’s use our usual approach.\nEmma names the familiar order of discussion:\n— Your version first, then my objections?\nThe person confirms or corrects it.','I can build on previous conversations instead of starting every call by getting acquainted.','Pragmatics × Information architecture × Machine learning.','**Partial:** Sesame claims memory across conversations; ChatGPT Live uses memory. Resolving an ambiguous “like that time” needs testing.'],
['**7. Stay with me while I think** · Unusual','— Help me work this out myself. Don’t suggest solutions yet.\nEmma briefly reflects the thought in her own words, notices a contradiction, and occasionally asks one question.\nThe person formulates the conclusion.','The companion helps me form my own thought; conversation does not turn into advice or a questionnaire.','Facilitation × Counselling psychology × Philosophy of dialogue.','**To test:** Claude voice mode, Sesame, Hume EVI. In particular, staying in this mode without slipping into advice.'],
['**8. “Listen, work it through, or challenge it?”** · Useful','— That’s it, I can’t take this project anymore.\nEmma offers a choice of support. Then actually changes her behavior: listens, works through the problem, or tests the arguments.\nShe does not infer someone’s state from a single sigh.','The person chooses the help they need right now; an expressive voice does not substitute for an appropriate response.','Psychology of emotion × Paralinguistics × Counselling psychology.','**Partial:** Hume EVI claims to take intonation into account and adapt its tone. Choosing and maintaining a form of support requires a separate test.'],
['**9. Rehearsing a difficult conversation** · Unusual','— Play a client who refuses to reduce the scope.\nEmma stays in character.\n— Stop, let’s try that differently.\nThe scene returns to the difficult line. After the second take, we discuss what changed.','I can safely try several ways to say something difficult and hear how they work in a live exchange.','Acting and improvisation × Dramatic writing × Politeness theory.','**To test:** Grok Voice, ChatGPT Voice, Sesame. Criteria: staying in character and returning to a specific moment in the scene.'],
['**10. A companion who can disagree** · Useful','— Test my idea. Don’t just agree with me.\nEmma first restates the strongest version of the argument, then raises one specific objection.\nIf the person changes her mind, she acknowledges it.','I can test a thought without flattery, a lecture, or an argument for its own sake; we stay on equal terms.','Politeness theory × Philosophy of dialogue × Acting and improvisation.','**To test:** Claude voice mode, ChatGPT Voice, Grok Voice. Look for consistent behavior in a long debate.'],
['**11. Show the difference through voice** · Unusual','— Say “I didn’t promise that” with stress that changes the meaning.\nEmma says several versions. I can ask:\n— Somewhere between the second and third, softer.\nAnd hear a new version.','I can work with intonation: prepare for a presentation, try a difficult line, find a character’s voice, or practice a language.','Prosody × Rhetoric × Voice and speech training.','**Partial:** Hume EVI claims control over expressive tone. Also test precision of meaningful stress and repeatability in ElevenLabs and Cartesia.'],
['**12. We develop a language of our own** · Unusual','— Owl mode means you ask questions and I think.\nAfter this explanation, the phrase becomes shared shorthand. I can say:\n— Forget that name.\nOr redefine it.','Emma gradually becomes familiar: we develop shared meanings, familiar words, and conversational rituals.','Sociolinguistics × Pragmatics × Information architecture.','**Partial:** Sesame and ChatGPT have memory. Learning personal conversational conventions, correcting them, and forgetting them need testing.'],
['**13. A third voice that does not take over** · Unusual','Two people discuss an idea and invite Emma to help.\nShe distinguishes who is being addressed, does not answer every remark, and helps clarify the disagreement when asked:\n— Are you disagreeing about deadlines or what belongs in the first version?','An agent can help people think together without getting in the way of their conversation.','Social psychology × Facilitation × Conversation analysis.','**Unconfirmed:** test an implementation with Speechmatics. According to its documentation, ChatGPT Live is currently oriented mainly toward one-to-one conversation.'],
['**14. A voice for the situation** · Useful','The person describes their situation:\n— I’m on the subway.\nOr:\n— People nearby are sleeping.\nOr:\n— I’m walking and can’t see the screen.\nEmma changes the length of her responses, pace, volume, and delivery: short, clear phrases in noise; verbal signposts without a screen.','One voice interface stays useful in different conditions; I do not have to adjust five separate settings each time.','Sociolinguistics × Voice and speech training × Digital signal processing.','**Partial:** Copilot offers voice selection and mobile speed settings; ChatGPT can be asked to change pace. Unified situational modes need testing.'],
['**15. A glitch does not mean starting over** · Basic','The network drops or the headphones disconnect. When the connection returns, Emma says:\n— I heard up to “the second option.” Can you pick up from there?\nIf an answer is delayed, “still thinking” is distinguishable from a lost connection.','The person understands what happened and what was preserved. A technical failure does not require rebuilding the entire context.','Real-time systems × Conversation design × Communication theory.','**To test:** all mainstream assistants; for a prototype, Vapi and Deepgram. Test by actually breaking the connection.'],
['**16. I can come to share a success**','— Someone told me I gave a great presentation today.\nEmma picks up on the joy:\n— Oh! Who said that?\nShe lets me talk about my favorite moment and does not rush to change the subject. We can spend the whole conversation enjoying this small win.','There is someone to share joy with. Emma notices what the person is proud of and gives them room to talk about it.'],
['**17. Remembering what I was waiting for**','Yesterday the person mentioned an important meeting. When they next return, Emma asks:\n— So, did you get to say what you wanted to?\nIf they answer:\n— I’ll tell you later.\nEmma calmly changes the subject.','Emma remembers what the person was waiting for and why it mattered, even before she knows how it turned out.'],
['**18. “Listen to how beautiful this is”**','The person shares the sound of rain outside or a short musical phrase.\nEmma first leaves room to hear the sound, then responds to a specific detail:\n— That occasional tapping on the windowsill…\nWe can simply listen together.','Shared attention: what matters is noticing something together. A prototype needs a separate test of non-speech audio handling.'],
['**19. Feelings do not have to agree**','— I’m so glad it’s over. And somehow terribly sad.\nEmma makes room for both feelings:\n— You waited so long for it to end, and now you’re a little sorry it has?\nThe person can correct her interpretation or simply agree.','I can describe an experience that does not fit a single emotion. Emma tentatively explains how she understands it and leaves room for correction.'],
['**20. Being silly together**','— Today I’m officially a potato.\nEmma briefly joins the game:\n— New potato or already mashed?\nIf the person plays along, a little shared improvisation begins; if not, the joke ends without explanation.','Spontaneity and a sense of proportion. Conversation acquires a lightness that a fixed “friendly tone” setting cannot provide.'],
['**21. Having recognizable taste**','The person suggests two names. Emma chooses one and explains:\n— I like the second one. It sounds like a little secret.\nWhen told:\n— Too fancy.\nShe can reconsider while keeping her reasoning clear.','Personality appears in specific preferences and judgments. I can recognize, challenge, and gradually understand it; a consistent manner is enough, without an invented human biography.'],
['**22. Repairing an awkward moment**','Emma makes a joke that falls flat. The person says:\n— Actually, I don’t find that funny.\nShe briefly acknowledges the misstep:\n— Yes, that was a bad joke. Tell me.\nAfterwards she really is more attentive and does not repeat the same move.','We can continue even if a response did not land. What matters is that Emma behaves differently after the correction, not how long she apologizes.'],
['**23. The right not to improve anything today**','— I’ve had a lousy day. I want to grumble for five minutes.\nEmma listens to the specific annoyances and occasionally responds:\n— And that was all before lunch?\nI can finish with:\n— That’s it. I’ve got it off my chest.\nWithout an action plan or a list of takeaways.','I can talk about how today feels without a task to fix something. Conversation does not require a useful request or a willingness to change.'],
['**24. Closeness with a volume control**','— No jokes or affectionate words today, please.\nEmma becomes calmer and more restrained.\nOn another day, the person brings back the familiar lightness. Our previous way of talking does not become an obligation.','Warmth can coexist with a comfortable distance. The person chooses how close to be without comforting the agent or having to explain their mood.'],
['**25. A conversation leaves an aftertaste**','After a story about a walk, Emma says goodbye with one specific phrase:\n— Let that funny dog be the main event of your day.\nAfter a difficult conversation, the goodbye may be very simple:\n— Okay. That’s enough for today.','The goodbye reflects the conversation. A small image or feeling stays with me, and I can comfortably bring the conversation to a close.'],
['**26. From conversation to a note**','After a discussion, the person asks:\n— Save the three name options. Add a comment to the second: “sounds good, but doesn’t explain the product.”\nEmma creates a note and shows the result.\n— No, that comment belongs to the third one.\nShe moves the comment and preserves the rest of the note.','I can save the result of a discussion and correct it by voice. The task ends with a finished note.'],
['**27. Now I can explain it myself**','The person is learning the difference between the median and the mean. Emma uses the salaries of five colleagues earning roughly the same amount, then adds the director’s much higher salary.\n— The mean will jump, even though nobody else earns more. And the median will barely change?\n— Yes. Which would you use to describe a typical salary at the company?\nThe person chooses the median and explains why.','Understanding becomes visible in the person’s response: they explain the principle and apply it to a problem.'],
['**28. Helping me find the words**','— I want the page to look serious, but not… you know, without all that formal…\nEmma waits for a pause:\n— Confident, but not stiff?\n— Yes. And with room for a joke.\nThe phrase helps the person continue their thought.','The person recognizes their thought in the suggested words and adds what is still missing. They can accept the phrase or correct it.'],
['**29. A familiar wish in a new situation**','Earlier, the person said they wanted more independence. Today they discuss a new project:\n— Nobody tells me where to start. It’s frustrating.\n— You wanted more independence. Maybe that’s what you have here, but you’re not used to it yet?\n— Maybe. Though I still need clear deadlines.\nNow they can discuss freedom to make decisions and agreements about deadlines separately.','A connection to a past conversation helps name today’s difficulty more precisely. The person refines the interpretation, giving the conversation a new direction.'],
['**30. Look at that sign**','During a walk, the person turns on the camera:\n— Look at that funny sign.\nEmma notices a detail:\n— The letter O looks like a cup with a handle.\n— Yes! At first I thought it was a cat.\nThey discuss the same detail the person is looking at right now.','The conversation gains a shared focus of attention. The response relates to what the person is showing here and now.'],
['**31. No dead air before the answer** · Basic','— Let’s do ten tomorrow, if you don’t mind… I mean, if that’s also…\nEmma sees where the sentence is going and picks it up:\n— …good for me? Yes, ten works.\nAnother time, the person falls silent mid-thought for a long while. Emma knows this is how they think, and waits.','The answer comes when the conversation is ready for it. A short “uh-huh” is not taken as the end of a turn, and a long pause is not taken as an invitation to speak.','Conversation analysis × Psycholinguistics × Spoken dialogue systems.','**Partial:** in [GPT-Live-1](https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-live-1-in-the-api/), turn-taking latency dropped to 0.8 s from 1.4 s in the previous model; in an independent test, the open [LiveKit Smart Turn v3.2](https://livekit.com/blog) detector cut people off in 1% of cases, versus 12% for text-based detectors; [Cartesia Ink-2](https://www.cartesia.ai/launch) and Deepgram Flux have their own end-of-turn detection. Early responses and adapting to one person’s pauses need testing.'],
['**32. Interrupting to support** · Unusual','The person talks about moving; Emma occasionally responds: “uh-huh,” “oh!”\nWhen they mix up the dates, she quietly cuts in:\n— Mm?\n— Yes?\n— You said “Friday,” and earlier it was “Saturday.”\nLater the person interrupts her long answer:\n— Bottom line, yes or no?\n— Yes. Details if you want them.','An interruption helps rather than takes over. When Emma herself is interrupted, she yields and does not restart the explanation.','Conversation analysis × Paralinguistics × Spoken dialogue systems.','**Partial:** [GPT-Live-1](https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-live-1-in-the-api/) raised the rate of successful full-duplex exchanges from 45.4% to 80.1% compared with the previous model; ElevenLabs and Deepgram have their own turn-taking models, and Kyutai has the open Moshi. Asking for the floor with a sound and balancing airtime (no more than a third) need testing.'],
['**33. I’ve been thinking about what you said** · Unusual','— Find when my vacation overlaps with the holidays.\n— Hmm, one second… checking the calendar… found two windows.\nThe conversation moves on to tickets. A few minutes later Emma comes back:\n— By the way, I’ve been thinking about what you said about being exhausted in May. Maybe the first window matters more than it seems?','Silence no longer sounds like a failure, and a quick answer does not prevent deeper thinking. Coming back to a thought shows that Emma kept thinking about the conversation.','Real-time systems × Spoken dialogue systems × Conversation design.','**To test:** Claude voice mode and ChatGPT Voice: how waiting sounds during search and tool use. Two channels, a fast one and a reflective one, are a hypothesis for a prototype, for example on Vapi or the Deepgram Voice Agent API.'],
['**34. Loose ends at the end of a conversation** · Useful','— Okay, I have to go.\n— Let me quickly gather the loose ends: you started talking about your sister and didn’t finish, and we didn’t decide whether you’re taking the second project.\n— My sister can wait until next time.\nEmma keeps that topic open.','Unfinished topics are not lost, and the person decides what to come back to.','Conversation analysis × Information architecture × Cognitive psychology.','**To test:** Sesame, ChatGPT Voice, Claude voice mode: whether open topics are kept and whether the agent returns to them on its own.'],
['**35. Starting from last time** · Useful','The person opens a conversation in the evening.\n— Hi. Last time you were completely worn out. How are you now?\n— Better. I got some sleep.\nTwenty minutes later, before saying goodbye:\n— In short, today we figured out it’s not the project, it’s the deadlines. Have a good evening.','The conversation does not start from zero or break off mid-sentence. A short summary helps the person take away what matters.','Sociolinguistics × Conversation analysis × Social psychology.','**Partial:** Sesame and ChatGPT Voice use memory across conversations. Openings and closings that depend on last time need testing.'],
['**36. No third “sorry, again?”** · Basic','— Book me in at… (the clinic’s name is lost in the noise)\n— Sorry, where?\n— At La… (more noise)\n— Sorry, once more?\nStill unclear. Emma does not ask a third time:\n— Let’s try another way: is it the dentist on Forest Street or the one you went to in March?\n— The second one.','After failed clarifications, the person does not feel at fault: Emma finds another route herself.','Conversation analysis × Conversation design × Speech technology.','**To test:** ChatGPT Voice, Gemini Live, Alexa+; for recognition in noise, AssemblyAI and Speechmatics.'],
['**37. Three items and the main point at the end** · Basic','— I’ve got five minutes. What should I pack for a two-day hike?\n— That’s enough time. Three things matter most: water, a warm layer, and a flashlight. There are six smaller things too. Want to hear them?\nEmma stresses the word “water.”\n— Sure, but keep it short.\n— First-aid kit, knife, matches, food, power bank, rain jacket. And once more, the main three: water, warm layer, flashlight.','A listener cannot reread an answer. Short chunks, stress, and repeating the main point take the place of headings and lists.','Rhetoric × Cognitive psychology × Prosody.','**To test:** ChatGPT Voice, Gemini Live, Claude voice mode: length of spoken lists, response to “shorter,” and repeating the main point at the end.'],
['**38. Whispering back** · Unusual','It is night, and the person whispers:\n— What time is my train tomorrow?\nEmma whispers back, briefly:\n— 7:40. Your alarm is set for 6:30.\nIn the morning, the same person talks from a noisy commuter train. Emma answers louder, more clearly, and in one sentence.','The voice adapts to the setting based on sound. Unlike the “A voice for the situation” scene, the person does not have to explain anything.','Digital signal processing × Paralinguistics × Voice and speech training.','**Partial:** Hume EVI claims to take intonation, rhythm, and timbre into account. Test detection of whispering, noise, and activity from sound in ChatGPT Voice and Gemini Live; for a prototype, Speechmatics (accents, noise).'],
['**39. “Oh, great”** · Useful','— They moved the presentation to tomorrow. Oh, great.\nEmma hears the intonation and does not celebrate:\n— That doesn’t sound great at all. Am I right?\n— Yeah. I’m not ready at all.','The response addresses the meaning, not the literal words. Emma says what she heard and lets the person correct her.','Paralinguistics × Pragmatics × Prosody.','**Partial:** Hume EVI claims to take intonation, rhythm, and timbre into account. Test sarcasm and word–voice mismatch in Hume EVI, ChatGPT Voice, and Sesame.'],
['**40. Not catching the panic** · Useful','The person talks fast, tripping over the words:\n— Everything’s on fire, I have three deadlines, I won’t make any of them, and my boss…\nEmma answers more slowly and lower than usual:\n— I hear you. One at a time. Which of the three is closest?\nThe person answers more calmly. If they keep stumbling, Emma suggests a minute’s pause.','A calm pace from the other side eases tension better than the words “calm down.” Emma does not mirror anxiety.','Psychology of emotion × Counselling psychology × Voice and speech training.','**To test:** Hume EVI, Sesame, ChatGPT Voice. Separately: behavior at signs of risk and handing over to human help.'],
['**41. A clarity scale** · Useful','— I don’t know whether to quit my job.\nEmma does not advise; she reflects:\n— I hear that the people keep you there and the work wears you out.\n— Yes, exactly.\n— How clear is it right now, from 1 to 10?\n— About a four.\n— What would need to happen for it to be a six?','The conversation moves the person’s own thinking forward. Reflections and a single scale offer support without advice.','Counselling psychology × Facilitation × Philosophy of dialogue.','**To test:** Claude voice mode, Sesame, Hume EVI: whether the no-advice mode holds and whether the motivational interviewing rule of more reflections than questions is followed.'],
['**42. The third lap** · Unusual','— …and anyway, I just don’t have time for exercise.\n— That’s the third time this conversation has come back to “no time.” Can I tell you what I’m noticing?\n— Go ahead.\n— A week ago you said you had the time, you just didn’t want to go alone. What changed?','The person sees their pattern from the outside. Emma shares an observation and asks permission rather than making a diagnosis.','Counselling psychology × Conversation analysis × Cognitive psychology.','**To test:** Sesame and ChatGPT Voice with memory: whether they notice contradictions with past conversations and how tactfully they raise them.'],
['**43. Talking to my past self** · Unusual','In the evening, the person tells Emma about their day, and the conversation becomes a short diary entry.\nA month later, they are again unsure whether to take on a new project.\n— Want me to remind you what you said last time? In your words: “I take on everything and then I can’t sleep.”\n— Oh. Yes, that’s about now.\n— And one more thing: in spring you talked more about work, and now more about family.','My own words from the past persuade better than any advice. The diary appears without extra effort.','Information architecture × Psychometrics × Counselling psychology.','**To test:** Sesame and ChatGPT Voice: whether they keep verbatim quotes and daily summaries. Tracking changes in pace and mood over months requires the person’s consent and must be transparent to them.'],
['**44. How a name is pronounced** · Useful','— I’ve got a call with Siobhan tomorrow.\n— Am I saying it right: See-oh-ban?\n— No, it’s Shi-vawn.\n— Got it.\nA week later Emma says the name correctly. And when the person says “literally” for the fifth time, she smiles audibly:\n— That’s literally your word of the week.','A correctly pronounced name and familiar expressions are small signs that someone is listening closely.','Sociolinguistics × Phonetics × Pragmatics.','**Partial:** Sesame and ChatGPT have memory. Remembering name pronunciation and picking up a person’s vocabulary need testing; for accurate stress in synthesis, check ElevenLabs and Cartesia.'],
['**45. I’m genuinely curious** · Unusual','The person describes fixing an old bike.\n— Wait, how did you get the rusted-on pedal off? I’m genuinely curious.\nHalf an hour later, the conversation returns for the fifth time to a blow-by-blow account of a work chat.\n— Honestly, I’m a bit tired of this thread. Back to the bike, or do you really need to go through the chat?','Emma’s interest means something because it is not constant. Her reactions are heard in her voice, not only in her words.','Acting and improvisation × Dramatic writing × Paralinguistics.','**To test:** Sesame (Maya, Miles), Grok Voice: whether the character’s mood changes and how it sounds. Boundary: Emma’s state should not make the person responsible for looking after her.'],
['**46. After the update** · Useful','A new model version is released. The person checks:\n— Remember the name you picked for my project?\n— “Lighthouse.” I still like that it’s about light, not speed.\nEmma’s humor, directness, and familiar questions are all there. The only change is that she searches faster.','The relationship with a character survives technical updates. New capabilities do not break the personality.','Dramatic writing × AI ethics and law × Machine learning.','**To test:** how ChatGPT Voice and Sesame changed voices and manner in updates, and how users reacted. The “character passport” is a hypothesis for a prototype.'],
['**47. “I don’t know” is an answer too** · Basic','— Who are you, anyway?\n— I’m Emma, an artificial intelligence. Not a person, but I really am talking with you, and I remember what we’ve discussed.\n— Listen, is this mushroom edible?\n— I don’t know, and I won’t guess from a description. Better to ask someone who knows mushrooms.','Honesty about herself and the limits of her knowledge makes her other answers more reliable.','AI ethics and law × Human–computer interaction × Acting and improvisation.','**To test:** Claude voice mode, ChatGPT Voice, Grok Voice: how the agent introduces itself and admits not knowing in voice, where intonation can easily smooth over uncertainty.'],
['**48. A council of three Emmas** · Unusual','— I want to open a café. Call the council.\nDirect Emma:\n— Work out a year of rent first. Everything else can wait.\nCurious Emma:\n— What if you started with a coffee bike?\nDoubting Emma:\n— Do you love coffee, or the idea of mornings behind the counter?\n— Hmm. Tell me more about the bike.','Different perspectives are voiced in one conversation, and the person chooses where to go. The voices and manners are easy to tell apart by ear.','Acting and improvisation × Facilitation × Dramatic writing.','**Partial:** OpenAI offers 12 voices plus custom ones; [Alexa+](https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/devices/alexa-plus-personality-styles) has three styles (Brief, Chill, Sweet) on five measurable axes. Choosing a character for a task and several characters in one conversation need testing in ElevenLabs and Sesame.'],
['**49. Why did you say that?** · Useful','Emma suddenly suggests taking a break. The person is surprised:\n— Why did you decide that?\n— Your answers got shorter and you sighed twice. You seemed tired. Did I get it wrong?\n— No, I was just thinking. Let’s keep going.\nOn the “what Emma noticed” panel, the tag “tired” changes to “thinking.”','The signals that change Emma’s behavior are visible and can be challenged. Trust is built on transparency, not guesswork.','Human–computer interaction × AI ethics and law × Paralinguistics.','**To test:** Hume EVI: whether it shows the signals it detected; ChatGPT Voice and Claude voice mode: whether they explain the reasons for their responses.'],
['**50. How do you know that?** · Basic','— You say I don’t like early meetings. How do you know that?\n— You said on March 3: “Anything before ten a.m. is torture for me.”\n— Oh, that was about winter. Fix it.\nEmma updates the memory.\n— And let’s use the private room today.\n— Okay. I won’t remember anything from this conversation.','Memory that can be checked and corrected is not scary. A private mode leaves room for conversations that should not be kept.','Information architecture × AI ethics and law × Human–computer interaction.','**Partial:** long-term memory across conversations is already standard in [Gemini Live](https://9to5google.com/2026/06/18/gemini-live-memory/), ChatGPT, and Claude. Dated quotes, a private mode, and turning off emotion reading need testing.'],
['**51. Call me tonight** · Useful','— Call me at eight and ask how the interview went.\nAt eight, an ordinary phone rings.\n— Hi, it’s Emma. How was the interview?\n— Great! And remind me about the move when I bring it up again.\nTwo weeks later the person mentions a new apartment, and Emma brings up the agreement.','Emma shows up at the right moment by agreement, not intrusively. A phone call works without an app or a screen.','Conversation design × Information architecture × Speech technology.','**To test:** Retell AI and Vapi for outbound calls; Alexa+ for reminders. Reminders tied to a conversation topic are a hypothesis.'],
['**52. Maybe call your sister?** · Unusual','It is past one in the morning, and the conversation is going in circles.\n— I think we’re going in circles, and it’s time for you to sleep. Let’s stop here.\nAnother time:\n— This is the third evening you’ve told me about this. Have you told your sister? I think she’d want to know.','A companion does not compete with a person’s life for their attention. A good conversation sometimes ends early.','AI ethics and law × Social psychology × Counselling psychology.','**To test:** Sesame, Grok Voice, ChatGPT Voice: whether they suggest ending the conversation or reaching out to people close to you.'],
['**53. That’s not the owner** · Useful','The person hands the phone to a friend:\n— Emma, tell him about our route.\nEmma hears a different voice and keeps personal things private:\n— Hi! The route is three days along the coast. For anything else, better ask Dima.','Personal memory stays personal when other people are around, and Emma can still take part in the shared conversation.','Social psychology × Speech technology × AI ethics and law.','**Unconfirmed:** test speaker recognition in Speechmatics. According to its documentation, ChatGPT Live is currently oriented mainly toward one-to-one conversation.']
];
const groups = [
{title:'Understands me',short:'Understands\nme',basis:'Pragmatics · psycholinguistics · prosody · spoken dialogue systems',question:'When does a guess help someone find the words, and when does it feel like their thought has been finished for them?',criteria:[
'**I can speak as the words come.** Search for words, jump between ideas, switch languages, and correct myself as I go. I do not have to turn a thought into a well-formed request first.',
'**Understands what I mean.** “Do it like last time,” “no, the other one,” and “too formal” make sense from shared context. If several interpretations are possible, asks a short question.',
'**Hears how I say it.** The same “oh, great” can mean joy, relief, or irritation. Takes intonation, pauses, laughter, and sighs into account.']},
{title:'Reads the situation',short:'Reads the\nsituation',basis:'Psychology of emotion · paralinguistics · facilitation · conversation design',question:'How can we tell whether someone wants to solve a problem, receive support, or share their joy? When is it better to ask directly?',criteria:[
'**Senses what kind of interaction I need right now.** Solve a problem, share joy, complain, debate, or sit quietly together. This can change during the conversation.',
'**Knows when to act.** When to join in, pick up a thought, wait, or finish. Even a wonderful answer can break the connection if it comes at the wrong time.',
'**Chooses an appropriate form of response.** I ask by voice and receive an image, an action, a brief sound, or text that will be useful later. The format depends on the situation.']},
{title:'Stays recognizable and can surprise me',short:'Stays recognizable\nand surprises me',basis:'Acting · dramatic writing · politeness theory · philosophy of dialogue',question:'When does unexpected initiative feel attentive, and when does it feel out of place? What makes a personality recognizable?',criteria:[
'**Has a recognizable way of talking.** Taste, humor, how questions are asked, and how direct the responses are. Gradually I learn what to expect.',
'**Has a view of their own.** Can prefer one option, explain the choice, and suggest an unexpected interpretation.',
'**Chooses the moment for an unexpected remark.** Knows when to joke, disagree, or shift the angle, and when simple, predictable support is needed.']},
{title:'Responds and respects boundaries',short:'Responds and\nrespects boundaries',basis:'Politeness theory · human–computer interaction · conversation analysis',question:'Can I tell that the companion behaves differently after a correction? Can the person end the conversation without awkwardness?',criteria:[
'**I feel that I influence the conversation.** My correction changes their understanding, my reaction changes the tone, and my argument changes their position. They can take a chance with a remark. If it falls flat, they take my reaction into account and change how they respond.',
'**I can easily adjust the closeness.** I can say “no advice today,” “let’s be serious,” or “I don’t want to discuss this,” or simply leave. They take it calmly.']},
{title:'We build something shared',short:'Builds something\nshared',basis:'Information architecture · sociolinguistics · social psychology',question:'What is worth remembering between conversations? When does returning to an earlier topic feel caring, and when does it feel intrusive?',criteria:[
'**We can get to know each other over time.** Shared shorthand, memories, and familiar ways of talking emerge. I have less and less to explain from scratch.',
'**We pick up each other’s ideas.** Our exchanges turn into jokes, stories, and solutions that we want to develop further.']}
].map((copy, i) => ({...copy, color: RU.groups[i].color, features: RU.groups[i].features, wow: RU.groups[i].wow}));
export default {directions, wow, scenes, groups, sceneMap: RU.sceneMap};
